/** Expand vol 1 part 3 - substantive relationship rewrite. Original 181-row inventory preserved. */
export default {"relationship":[{"a":"If you are always guessing where you stand, ask for a clear conversation rather than building a future from mixed signals.","b":"A relationship should not require detective work; explain what you need to know and judge the answer alongside their consistent behaviour."},{"a":"Before calling it commitment, agree on what commitment means to both of you: exclusivity, time, communication, and shared responsibility.","b":"Do not assume you share the same definition of a serious relationship; name the expectations and check that both people genuinely accept them."},{"a":"When one person keeps cancelling plans, describe the pattern and its impact instead of arguing over one missed date.","b":"Ask whether the repeated cancellations reflect a real constraint or a lack of priority, then decide what level of reliability you can accept."},{"a":"If you are the only one initiating contact, pause the chase and explain that mutual effort matters to you.","b":"Rather than testing them with silence, tell them you want communication to feel reciprocal and observe whether they make room for it."},{"a":"A delayed reply is not automatically disrespect; look at the broader pattern and whether expectations about availability were ever discussed.","b":"If response times leave you anxious, agree on realistic communication habits instead of treating every gap as evidence of rejection."},{"a":"When jealousy appears, identify the specific behaviour that unsettled you and discuss it without demanding control over your partner.","b":"You can ask for reassurance and honesty, but trust cannot be built through surveillance, password demands, or restrictions on friendships."},{"a":"An apology matters when it names the harm, accepts responsibility, and is followed by a change you can actually observe.","b":"If the same hurt keeps recurring, stop measuring the relationship by how emotional the apology sounds and look at whether the pattern changes."},{"a":"During conflict, address one issue at a time; bringing every old disagreement into the room makes repair much harder.","b":"If the conversation becomes a catalogue of past mistakes, pause and agree on the single decision or behaviour you need to resolve first."},{"a":"Taking space during an argument can help, provided you say when you will return to the conversation rather than disappearing.","b":"A pause should regulate the discussion, not punish the other person; set a realistic time to reconnect and follow through."},{"a":"If your partner dismisses your feelings, explain what you experienced and what kind of response would help you feel heard.","b":"You do not need them to agree with every interpretation, but a healthy conversation requires curiosity rather than ridicule or contempt."},{"a":"A boundary describes what you will do to protect your wellbeing; it is not a rule that gives you ownership of another adult.","b":"Make boundaries specific and enforceable: state what behaviour you cannot continue around and what step you will take if it happens again."},{"a":"Do not use a breakup threat to win an argument; raise the possibility of ending things only when you are prepared to discuss it honestly.","b":"Repeated threats create insecurity without solving the problem; talk directly about whether the relationship can meet both people's needs."},{"a":"If you disagree about children, marriage, or where to live, treat those differences as major compatibility questions rather than minor details.","b":"Love does not remove the need for shared direction; discuss non-negotiable life plans early enough for both people to make informed choices."},{"a":"Money conversations should include debt, spending habits, savings goals, and financial obligations without turning income into a measure of worth.","b":"Before combining finances, agree on transparency, personal spending freedom, shared bills, and how decisions will be made when you disagree."},{"a":"When family members interfere, decide together what information stays private and who will communicate the boundary to relatives.","b":"Your partner should not have to choose between you and their family, but the two of you do need a united approach to intrusive behaviour."},{"a":"Long-distance relationships need practical agreements about visits, communication, costs, and what progress toward living nearer might look like.","b":"Do not let a long-distance promise remain indefinite; discuss a realistic timeline and revisit it when work, money, or family circumstances change."},{"a":"If affection has changed, ask about stress, health, emotional distance, or unmet needs before assuming the relationship has lost its value.","b":"Talk about the change without pressuring your partner to perform affection; both people deserve honesty and room to discuss what feels different."},{"a":"Consent and affection remain choices inside a relationship; being together never creates an obligation to intimacy.","b":"If either person is uncomfortable, stop and listen without bargaining, guilt, or claims that a partner owes physical access."},{"a":"If trust was broken, agree on what repair requires and how long you both are willing to work toward it.","b":"Rebuilding trust is not the same as demanding instant forgiveness; the person who caused harm must show sustained accountability."},{"a":"Do not confuse checking someone's location with emotional security; discuss the fear underneath the request before making monitoring a habit.","b":"Voluntary sharing can be useful for practical safety, but constant tracking should not become a condition for being believed or loved."},{"a":"When a partner shares something vulnerable, ask whether they want advice, practical help, or simply someone to listen.","b":"Support often starts with making room for their experience instead of rushing to defend yourself or solve the problem immediately."},{"a":"If you feel you have outgrown the relationship, be honest about the change rather than staying while quietly withdrawing.","b":"A difficult truth delivered respectfully is kinder than keeping someone invested in a future you no longer intend to build."},{"a":"Compatibility includes how you handle stress, disagreement, responsibility, and ordinary routines, not only how exciting the early stage feels.","b":"Notice how the relationship functions on difficult days; chemistry is meaningful, but daily respect and reliability sustain partnership."},{"a":"When your partner succeeds, celebrate without turning their progress into a competition or a threat to your own identity.","b":"A supportive relationship makes room for two ambitions; discuss how to share time and responsibilities as either person's opportunities grow."},{"a":"If one person carries most domestic work, list the recurring tasks and divide ownership rather than waiting for the other to notice.","b":"Fairness is not only about helping when asked; both partners should take responsibility for planning, remembering, and completing shared work."},{"a":"Do not assume a partner can read your mind; make a clear request and give them a fair chance to respond.","b":"Unspoken expectations often become resentment; explain what would make you feel supported and ask what they need in return."},{"a":"If you keep arguing about the same issue, identify the unmet need beneath it and agree on one behaviour each person can change.","b":"A recurring argument may signal an unresolved decision; stop replaying the fight and define what a workable agreement would look like."},{"a":"You can forgive someone and still decide that continuing the relationship is not right for you.","b":"Forgiveness is a personal process, not a contract to restore access; choose reconciliation only if safety, trust, and willingness are present."},{"a":"If a partner insults, intimidates, isolates, or controls you, treat the pattern seriously and seek support from someone you trust.","b":"Disagreement is normal; fear and coercion are not. Prioritise your safety and consider specialist support if you feel threatened or trapped."},{"a":"When ending a relationship, be clear and respectful; avoid offering false hope just to soften a decision you have already made.","b":"A breakup does not require a debate in which the other person must approve your choice; communicate the decision safely and hold the boundary."},{"a":"If your partner needs more time before defining the relationship, ask what they are deciding and when you can revisit the conversation.","b":"You can respect uncertainty without waiting indefinitely; decide what timeframe feels fair for you and communicate it calmly."},{"a":"Being exclusive should be a mutual agreement, not something inferred from frequent dates, affection, or assumptions.","b":"Ask directly whether you are both seeing other people and agree on what exclusivity means before either person relies on it."},{"a":"If someone says they are not ready for a relationship, take the statement seriously instead of treating patience as a strategy to change their mind.","b":"You may care about them and still choose distance when your intentions differ; protect your time rather than accepting an arrangement you do not want."},{"a":"A healthy relationship does not require identical personalities; it requires respect for differences and workable agreements around them.","b":"Notice whether your differences can coexist without one person repeatedly shrinking their needs to keep the peace."},{"a":"If your partner makes jokes at your expense, explain which comments cross the line and ask them to stop.","b":"Humour should not depend on humiliating one person; repeated dismissal after you raise the issue is a sign to reassess respect."},{"a":"When a disagreement happens in public, avoid escalating for an audience and agree to continue privately when you are both calmer.","b":"You can say that the issue matters and still refuse to argue in front of friends, colleagues, or family members."},{"a":"Do not recruit friends to pressure your partner; seek perspective without turning private conflict into a public campaign.","b":"If you need outside support, choose someone discreet and fair who can help you think clearly rather than inflame the disagreement."},{"a":"If your partner asks for privacy around a personal matter, clarify what can be shared and what must remain between you.","b":"Privacy is not automatically secrecy; agree on boundaries that protect dignity while still allowing honest conversations about matters that affect you both."},{"a":"Social media attention is not a reliable measure of commitment; discuss the behaviour that actually affects trust between you.","b":"Rather than arguing over likes and follows in isolation, agree on what online conduct feels respectful to both of you."},{"a":"If an ex remains in your partner's life, discuss the nature of the contact and any boundaries needed for trust.","b":"A past relationship does not automatically make someone a threat; focus on honesty, transparency, and whether current agreements are respected."},{"a":"If your partner compares you with an ex, tell them how the comparison affects you and ask for the conversation to stay focused on your relationship.","b":"You are not competing with someone's history; a partner should address present needs without using another person as a measuring stick."},{"a":"A relationship can survive disagreement about religion or values only when both people can speak freely and respect each other's convictions.","b":"Discuss the practical impact of differing beliefs, including family expectations and future decisions, before assuming love will settle every conflict."},{"a":"If cultural expectations shape your relationship, distinguish the values you personally hold from pressures you feel obliged to obey.","b":"Talk about which traditions matter to each of you and create agreements that respect both partners rather than defaulting to one family's rules."},{"a":"When work consumes most of your partner's time, explain the specific connection you miss and propose time you can protect together.","b":"Ambition may require demanding seasons, but the two of you should agree on how to stay connected and when the arrangement will be reviewed."},{"a":"If you are carrying the emotional planning for every date and difficult conversation, name the imbalance and ask for shared initiative.","b":"Partnership includes noticing and organising; ask your partner to own some of the planning instead of waiting for instructions each time."},{"a":"A thoughtful gift cannot replace a conversation about a recurring problem; acknowledge the gesture and return to the issue that needs repair.","b":"Appreciate affection in the form it arrives, but do not let presents become a substitute for accountability or changed behaviour."},{"a":"If your partner apologises but asks you to move on immediately, explain that trust may need time and consistent actions to recover.","b":"You can recognise a sincere apology without pretending the impact has disappeared; agree on practical steps that help repair the harm."},{"a":"When you need reassurance, make a direct request rather than setting traps to see whether your partner notices your distress.","b":"Tell them what reassurance looks like in practice, then discuss whether that request is realistic and sustainable for both of you."},{"a":"If your partner is quiet after a difficult day, ask whether they want company or time alone instead of assuming the silence is about you.","b":"Give space without withdrawing care; a simple check-in can show support while respecting their need to decompress."},{"a":"Do not treat every difference in libido as rejection; discuss desire, stress, health, and affection without assigning blame.","b":"There is no requirement for partners to want intimacy at the same frequency; look for mutually comfortable ways to stay close without pressure."},{"a":"If you feel pressured to prove love through sex, money, access, or constant availability, name the pressure and seek support if it continues.","b":"Affection should be freely given; a partner who makes love conditional on compliance is not respecting your autonomy."},{"a":"When a partner is dealing with grief or illness, ask what support is useful and recognise that their capacity may change from day to day.","b":"You can be compassionate while also being honest about your own limits; sustainable care needs communication rather than silent self-sacrifice."},{"a":"If mental health struggles are affecting the relationship, encourage appropriate support while avoiding the role of sole therapist or rescuer.","b":"Care and patience matter, but both people need support systems; discuss what you can offer and where professional help may be needed."},{"a":"When a partner shares a concern, reflect what you heard before explaining your own perspective so they know you understood the issue.","b":"Listening does not mean surrendering your view; it means giving the other person a fair hearing before working toward a response."},{"a":"If you disagree about how often to see each other, compare schedules and needs instead of labelling one person clingy or distant.","b":"Find a rhythm that allows connection and independence; neither constant access nor emotional absence should be the default."},{"a":"A partner's friendships are part of their independent life; discuss genuine concerns without demanding that they cut people off to prove loyalty.","b":"If a friendship repeatedly crosses an agreed boundary, address the specific conduct together rather than issuing blanket bans."},{"a":"When you meet each other's friends, avoid using the occasion to test loyalty or force instant closeness.","b":"Let relationships develop naturally; basic courtesy and respect matter more than making everyone approve of you immediately."},{"a":"If you are unsure whether to move in together, discuss chores, money, privacy, guests, conflict, and what happens if the arrangement ends.","b":"Living together is a practical commitment as well as a romantic milestone; make the everyday expectations explicit before signing or moving."},{"a":"Before getting engaged, talk through finances, children, location, caregiving, values, and conflict habits rather than relying on the proposal moment.","b":"A proposal should confirm a decision already explored together; use premarital conversations to uncover differences while you can still choose freely."},{"a":"If your partner avoids every conversation about the future, explain why clarity matters and ask whether they genuinely want the same direction.","b":"You cannot force readiness, but you can decide how long you are willing to remain in a relationship with no shared plan."},{"a":"If you discover that your goals have changed, tell your partner before resentment turns the difference into distance.","b":"People can grow in different directions; discuss what has changed and whether a shared future still makes sense for both of you."},{"a":"A relationship should leave room for friendships, interests, and time alone; closeness does not require giving up your separate identity.","b":"If independence feels threatening to either of you, discuss the fear underneath it and agree on healthy time together and apart."},{"a":"When your partner needs a night with friends, support their independence and keep your own plans meaningful too.","b":"Trust is strengthened when both people can enjoy separate lives without punishment, suspicion, or scorekeeping."},{"a":"If you feel excluded from important decisions, explain which decisions affect you and ask to be included before plans are finalised.","b":"Not every choice needs joint approval, but shared commitments deserve a process in which both partners have a voice."},{"a":"When one person relocates for the relationship, discuss work, community, finances, and support rather than treating the move as a simple romantic gesture.","b":"A major sacrifice should be planned together; agree on how the relocating partner will build an independent life and what support the other will provide."},{"a":"If a career opportunity requires time apart, compare the opportunity's benefits with the relationship's practical needs before making promises.","b":"Avoid framing the decision as proof of love; explore options, timelines, costs, and whether both people can accept the likely trade-offs."},{"a":"If you are struggling with your partner's success, be honest about insecurity and work on it without asking them to become smaller.","b":"Your partner can support you, but they should not have to hide achievements to protect your confidence; build your own goals alongside theirs."},{"a":"When a partner loses a job, separate their current employment status from their value and discuss a realistic plan for the next steps.","b":"Offer encouragement without taking over; agree on practical help, household adjustments, and how to talk about financial pressure respectfully."},{"a":"If one partner earns more, decide together how shared costs will be divided in a way that reflects income and fairness.","b":"Equal partnership does not always mean identical contributions; make the arrangement transparent and revisit it when circumstances change."},{"a":"Before lending money to a partner, be clear whether it is a gift or a loan and what repayment would realistically involve.","b":"Money can complicate affection; do not lend what you cannot afford to lose, and put important agreements in plain language."},{"a":"If you disagree about supporting relatives financially, set a shared budget and discuss obligations before money leaves joint accounts.","b":"Family care can be important, but neither partner should make major financial commitments on behalf of the household without agreement."},{"a":"When household work feels unfair, track the invisible tasks too: planning meals, remembering appointments, shopping, and organising repairs.","b":"A fair division includes mental load as well as visible chores; assign full ownership so one person is not managing the other person's help."},{"a":"If parenting responsibilities are uneven, discuss specific routines and handovers rather than arguing that one person never helps.","b":"Children benefit when caregiving is reliable; agree on who owns each task and how you will adjust when work or health changes."},{"a":"If you disagree about parenting discipline, discuss the values and outcomes you want before correcting each other in front of the children.","b":"Present a calm, consistent approach where possible, then resolve differences privately without undermining one another's role."},{"a":"When blending families, give children time to adjust and avoid demanding instant affection or authority from a new partner.","b":"Build trust through predictable care and respectful boundaries; the adults should agree on discipline and parental roles before conflict arises."},{"a":"If fertility or family planning becomes difficult, approach the subject with compassion and avoid treating either partner as the problem.","b":"Medical and reproductive decisions can carry grief and pressure; seek qualified care together if wanted, while respecting each person's autonomy."},{"a":"If you disagree about having children, do not assume one person will eventually change their mind; discuss the difference honestly.","b":"This is a core life decision, not a compromise that can be imposed; both partners deserve clarity before making long-term commitments."},{"a":"When caring for an ageing relative affects your time together, plan responsibilities and respite instead of expecting one partner to absorb everything.","b":"Caregiving can reshape a household; discuss limits, finances, outside help, and how to protect the relationship from becoming only a logistics team."},{"a":"If your partner has a disability or chronic condition, ask what support they want rather than assuming you know what is best.","b":"Respect autonomy while discussing practical adjustments; support should be collaborative, not controlling or based on pity."},{"a":"When your own needs are not being met, state them clearly and ask whether your partner is willing and able to work on them.","b":"You can make a reasonable request, but you cannot negotiate someone into wanting the same relationship; their response gives you information."},{"a":"If your partner says they feel unheard, ask for a recent example and listen for the pattern instead of debating their wording.","b":"Take the feedback seriously without accepting unfair accusations wholesale; identify what you can change and explain where your view differs."},{"a":"When you feel criticised, separate the specific feedback from the way it was delivered and ask for a more constructive conversation.","b":"You can own a mistake without accepting insults; request respectful language and return to the issue once both people are ready."},{"a":"If arguments involve shouting, agree on a pause before either person says something harmful, then return to the topic at a set time.","b":"A calm-down break works only when it is mutual and followed by repair; it should never be used to silence the other person indefinitely."},{"a":"Do not demand that your partner discuss a painful subject on your schedule; agree on a time when both of you can participate safely.","b":"Avoidance cannot solve a recurring issue, but forcing an immediate conversation may make it worse; set a clear time to return to it."},{"a":"If your partner refuses all accountability, describe the repeated impact and decide what boundary protects you if nothing changes.","b":"You can invite repair, but you cannot do both people's work; a relationship needs willingness from each person to address harm."},{"a":"When a disagreement has no perfect solution, look for an arrangement that protects each person's core needs rather than declaring a winner.","b":"Compromise should not mean one person always gives way; alternate flexibility and check whether the outcome feels sustainable to both."},{"a":"If you need to revisit an agreement, explain what changed and ask to renegotiate instead of quietly breaking the original understanding.","b":"Agreements are not permanent if circumstances change, but trust depends on raising the change before acting on it."},{"a":"When a partner makes a mistake in front of others, avoid humiliating them; discuss the impact privately unless immediate safety requires intervention.","b":"Respect does not mean concealing serious harm; distinguish an ordinary error from conduct that needs to be addressed openly."},{"a":"If your partner shares private information without permission, explain the breach and agree on what remains confidential going forward.","b":"Trust requires care with personal stories; ask before sharing sensitive details with friends or family, even when you want advice."},{"a":"When you are unsure about a relationship label, ask what the label would change in daily expectations rather than debating the word alone.","b":"Names matter when they clarify commitment; agree on the responsibilities and boundaries behind the label you choose."},{"a":"If you suspect dishonesty, identify what you know and ask a direct question instead of presenting guesses as proven facts.","b":"A difficult answer may hurt, but accusations built on assumptions rarely create clarity; explain the inconsistency and listen to the response."},{"a":"If your partner admits to lying, discuss what was hidden, why it happened, and what would make honesty possible from here.","b":"One confession does not automatically restore trust; look for transparency and changed choices over time before deciding what comes next."},{"a":"Do not ask for access to every private message as a shortcut to trust; agree on honest behaviour without erasing personal privacy.","b":"Transparency should be voluntary and proportionate to the issue being repaired, not an open-ended entitlement to inspect someone's private life."},{"a":"If you discover a betrayal, give yourself time to process before making a decision under pressure from your partner or other people.","b":"You do not owe an immediate answer about staying or leaving; seek support, clarify what happened, and choose at a pace that feels safe."},{"a":"If you choose to rebuild after infidelity, define what accountability and boundaries mean instead of relying on vague promises.","b":"Reconciliation is possible only if both people freely want it; the person who broke trust must accept that repair takes sustained effort."},{"a":"If you decide to leave after betrayal, you can end the relationship without proving that your pain is valid to everyone.","b":"You may explain your decision once, but you do not need to keep defending a boundary to people who minimise what happened."},{"a":"If a partner repeatedly flirts in ways that violate your agreement, discuss the exact behaviour and whether your expectations are shared.","b":"Do not argue about a universal definition of flirting; decide together what conduct crosses the line in your relationship."},{"a":"When you feel compared with someone else, tell your partner that comparisons undermine connection and ask to discuss your relationship on its own terms.","b":"You deserve to be treated as an individual, not a contestant; if comparisons continue after you raise the harm, take the pattern seriously."},{"a":"If your partner makes decisions about your shared home without consulting you, explain which choices require joint agreement.","b":"A shared space should reflect both people's needs; agree on spending limits and decisions that need discussion before action."},{"a":"When guests or relatives stay over, agree on notice, duration, costs, and private space so hospitality does not become resentment.","b":"Being welcoming does not mean surrendering your comfort; both partners should have a voice in who enters the home and for how long."},{"a":"If you disagree about pets, travel, or lifestyle choices, discuss daily responsibilities and costs rather than focusing only on the exciting part.","b":"Shared decisions should account for the less glamorous work too; make sure both people accept the ongoing commitment before agreeing."},{"a":"When planning a holiday, talk about budget, pace, activities, and downtime so one person's idea of rest does not become another's stress.","b":"A good trip makes room for different preferences; divide planning and agree on spending before bookings create pressure."},{"a":"If your partner forgets an important date, explain why it mattered and discuss how you both prefer to mark meaningful occasions.","b":"A forgotten date may be an oversight rather than a verdict on love; focus on the meaning and the effort you want going forward."},{"a":"If celebrations matter more to you than to your partner, explain what makes them meaningful and find a form you can both enjoy.","b":"Different traditions are workable when neither person mocks the other's needs; agree on a gesture that feels sincere rather than forced."},{"a":"When affection is expressed differently, learn what makes each of you feel cared for instead of insisting that one style is the only valid one.","b":"Ask for specific actions that help you feel loved and stay curious about your partner's preferences; care is clearer when it is concrete."},{"a":"If physical touch is uncomfortable at a particular time, say so without apologising for having a boundary.","b":"A partner can feel disappointed and still respect your no; affection should never be extracted through guilt or persistence."},{"a":"If your partner is not ready to introduce you to family, ask what is behind the hesitation and what timeline feels realistic.","b":"You can respect privacy while still asking whether the relationship is moving toward the openness and recognition you need."},{"a":"If you are kept secret in ways that make you feel hidden, explain the difference between privacy and being denied acknowledgement.","b":"Ask what the secrecy is protecting and for how long; decide whether the explanation fits your values and circumstances."},{"a":"When a relationship is public online but absent from real life, discuss what visibility means to each of you rather than demanding a post.","b":"Social media cannot certify commitment; focus on whether you are included, respected, and treated consistently in the parts of life that matter."},{"a":"If your partner posts private arguments online, explain the impact and agree that conflict should not become public entertainment.","b":"Do not retaliate with your own post; protect your privacy and address whether public shaming is becoming a pattern."},{"a":"When you need advice about your relationship, share only what is necessary and protect details your partner reasonably expects to remain private.","b":"Choose a trusted person who can offer perspective without spreading the story; support-seeking should not become public exposure."},{"a":"If your partner's friends are disrespectful toward you, describe the specific incidents and ask your partner to address them.","b":"You do not need every friend to like you, but your partner should not dismiss repeated disrespect or leave you to manage it alone."},{"a":"If you dislike one of your partner's friends, focus on conduct that affects the relationship rather than demanding a friendship ban.","b":"You can express concern and set limits around shared time, but your partner retains the right to choose their own friendships."},{"a":"When a friend gives your partner relationship advice, ask how the advice is affecting your decisions rather than attacking the friend.","b":"Outside opinions can be useful, but the two of you remain responsible for your agreements and should discuss decisions directly."},{"a":"If your partner discusses your disagreements with family, agree on what support is appropriate and what details should stay private.","b":"Family support can help, but involving relatives in every conflict may make repair harder; set boundaries around who gets brought into the issue."},{"a":"If a family member insults your partner, address the behaviour clearly instead of expecting your partner to tolerate it for family harmony.","b":"You can care about your relatives and still insist on respectful treatment; explain the boundary and follow through if it is ignored."},{"a":"When family expectations conflict with your plans as a couple, decide together which commitments are yours to make and which are optional.","b":"Tradition deserves thoughtful consideration, not automatic control; build a shared position before responding to family pressure."},{"a":"If one partner feels culturally misunderstood, ask them to explain what matters rather than treating the difference as unreasonable.","b":"Curiosity is more useful than defensiveness; learn the context behind a practice and decide together how it fits your shared life."},{"a":"When language differences cause misunderstandings, slow down, check what each person meant, and avoid mocking mistakes.","b":"Agree on ways to clarify tone and meaning, especially during conflict; patience helps prevent small translation gaps from becoming personal attacks."},{"a":"If your partner has a different conflict style, agree on a process that gives both people time to speak and time to reflect.","b":"One person may need to talk immediately while the other needs a pause; design a fair return-to-conversation plan instead of labelling either style wrong."},{"a":"If your partner shuts down during conflict, ask what helps them re-engage and agree on a time to continue the discussion.","b":"Silence may be a stress response or avoidance; explain that the issue still needs attention and make a concrete plan to return to it."},{"a":"If you become overwhelmed in an argument, say you need a break and specify when you can continue.","b":"Taking responsibility for your own regulation is useful, but make sure the pause does not become a way to avoid every difficult conversation."},{"a":"When a disagreement turns personal, bring it back to the behaviour or decision instead of attacking someone's character.","b":"You can be firm about the impact without using labels; respectful language makes it easier to solve the actual problem."},{"a":"If your partner interrupts repeatedly, ask for uninterrupted time to finish your point and offer them the same courtesy.","b":"Agree on a simple way to share the floor when conversations get heated; being heard should not depend on speaking the loudest."},{"a":"If you find yourself keeping score, talk about the unmet need behind the tally rather than listing every favour you have done.","b":"Shared effort is not a ledger, but a persistent imbalance deserves attention; discuss responsibilities and what each person can realistically contribute."},{"a":"When one partner is more organised, avoid letting that person become the default manager of the entire household.","b":"Make planning visible and divide ownership; remembering every deadline is work, even when the task itself looks small."},{"a":"If you feel taken for granted, describe the ordinary contributions that go unnoticed and ask for a more appreciative pattern.","b":"Appreciation is most meaningful when it is specific and consistent; tell your partner what recognition looks like to you."},{"a":"If your partner says they feel unappreciated, ask which actions would help rather than defending yourself with a list of what you already do.","b":"You may have different ways of showing care; find concrete gestures that register for them while sharing what makes you feel valued too."},{"a":"When your partner is under pressure, offer practical help but ask before taking over tasks they may want to handle themselves.","b":"Support should reduce the load without removing agency; ask what would genuinely help and respect the answer."},{"a":"If your partner refuses help, respect their choice while making it clear that support remains available when they want it.","b":"Do not turn assistance into proof of love; people can need care and still want control over how they manage their own situation."},{"a":"If you are always the one initiating repair after arguments, tell your partner that reconciliation needs effort from both sides.","b":"Do not confuse being first to apologise with being solely responsible; agree on how each of you can take part in making things right."},{"a":"When you have hurt your partner, avoid explaining your intention before acknowledging the impact they experienced.","b":"You can clarify what you meant after you have shown that you understand why the action hurt; intent does not erase consequences."},{"a":"If your partner apologises for something small, accept the repair when it feels genuine instead of extending the conflict to win leverage.","b":"Healthy accountability includes allowing mistakes to be repaired; do not use old errors as permanent ammunition after an issue is resolved."},{"a":"When the same apology is repeated without change, tell your partner what behaviour needs to be different and what you will do if it continues.","b":"Words can begin repair, but only changed behaviour sustains it; decide what evidence of progress would be meaningful to you."},{"a":"If you are unsure whether a relationship can recover, consider safety, honesty, willingness, and whether the original harm is still happening.","b":"Do not stay only because of the time already invested; assess the relationship as it is now and the future both people are prepared to build."},{"a":"If you feel lonely while in the relationship, describe when that feeling is strongest and what connection you are missing.","b":"Being partnered does not guarantee emotional closeness; ask whether both of you are willing to create regular, attentive time together."},{"a":"When you need more quality time, propose a specific routine that fits both schedules instead of making a broad accusation.","b":"A predictable shared ritual can help, but it should feel mutual; ask what kind of time together your partner would also enjoy."},{"a":"If your partner prefers quiet time while you prefer busy social plans, alternate activities and protect room for both preferences.","b":"Neither person should have to perform a lifestyle they dislike; find a rhythm that includes shared experiences and independent choices."},{"a":"If your partner is introverted, do not interpret every request for solitude as a lack of affection.","b":"Explain your need for connection while respecting their energy limits; agree on signals that distinguish a recharge break from emotional withdrawal."},{"a":"If you need more verbal affection, tell your partner what words make a difference rather than assuming they should know.","b":"People express care differently, but a small effort to speak in a way your partner understands can strengthen emotional connection."},{"a":"When your partner is not verbally expressive, look at their broader behaviour while still being honest about what you miss.","b":"Do not dismiss your own need for reassurance; explore whether both of you can adapt without pretending to be someone else."},{"a":"If physical affection changes after a stressful period, ask what is happening and make space for an answer without pressure.","b":"Stress, health, grief, and conflict can affect closeness; approach the change as something to understand together, not a debt to collect."},{"a":"If you want to rebuild romance, discuss what each of you finds meaningful instead of relying on grand gestures copied from elsewhere.","b":"Small, attentive actions often matter more than expensive surprises; choose gestures that reflect your actual shared life."},{"a":"When planning a date, share the effort and consider each person's budget, energy, and interests.","b":"Romance should not become another invisible task for one partner; take turns suggesting plans and make low-cost time together count."},{"a":"If you cannot afford the lifestyle your partner expects, discuss your actual finances rather than borrowing to maintain appearances.","b":"A relationship should allow honest limits; agree on experiences that fit your means and avoid turning spending into a test of devotion."},{"a":"If your partner criticises your body, tell them clearly that comments about your appearance are hurting you and must stop.","b":"You deserve respect at every size and stage of life; repeated body-shaming is not constructive feedback and should not be normalised."},{"a":"When either partner experiences a major life change, make room to renegotiate routines and expectations instead of assuming the old arrangement still fits.","b":"A relationship needs adjustment as people change; check in about time, support, responsibilities, and what each person can manage now."},{"a":"If you are struggling to balance a new baby and your partnership, plan small moments of connection without demanding that life return to its old pace.","b":"The early parenting period can be exhausting; share the load, seek practical help where possible, and speak kindly about what both of you are managing."},{"a":"When a child leaves home or a major caregiving role ends, discuss how you want to reconnect and what new routines might look like.","b":"A life transition can expose distance that was hidden by busyness; approach it with curiosity and create shared goals for this next stage."},{"a":"If a partner is grieving, do not set a deadline for them to feel normal; ask what presence and practical support would help today.","b":"Grief can change communication and energy for a long time; stay compassionate while being honest about when additional support is needed."},{"a":"If your relationship has become mostly logistics, choose a regular time to talk about each other rather than only schedules and chores.","b":"Practical teamwork matters, but emotional connection needs attention too; ask what helps each of you feel like partners rather than co-managers."},{"a":"When you are considering counselling, frame it as a structured space to understand patterns, not a courtroom to prove who is wrong.","b":"A qualified couples counsellor can help when both people are willing; if there is abuse or coercive control, seek individual specialist support first."},{"a":"If your partner refuses counselling, explain why you think support could help and decide what options remain available to you.","b":"You cannot force someone into therapy; individual counselling can still help you clarify boundaries, choices, and the support you need."},{"a":"If conflict includes threats, intimidation, physical harm, or control, prioritise safety rather than trying to fix communication as a couple.","b":"Abuse is not a mutual communication problem; seek confidential specialist help and make a safety plan that fits your circumstances."},{"a":"If you feel afraid to disagree with your partner, take that fear seriously and speak privately with someone you trust.","b":"A relationship should allow honest disagreement without retaliation; consider specialist support if you are being monitored, isolated, or threatened."},{"a":"If your partner threatens self-harm to stop you leaving, contact emergency or crisis support rather than accepting sole responsibility for their safety.","b":"Take threats seriously, involve qualified help, and protect your own safety; you are not required to remain in a relationship under coercion."},{"a":"When you end a relationship, plan the conversation and practical arrangements with safety in mind, especially if you expect an aggressive reaction.","b":"You may choose a public place, remote message, or support person if that is safer; there is no obligation to break up face-to-face."},{"a":"If you share a home and decide to separate, plan housing, belongings, finances, and essential documents before making rushed moves.","b":"A separation plan should account for safety and legal obligations; seek local professional advice where property, children, or immigration status are involved."},{"a":"If children are involved in a separation, keep adult conflict away from them and make practical arrangements focused on their stability.","b":"Do not use children as messengers or leverage; seek appropriate family guidance and prioritise predictable, safe caregiving."},{"a":"If your partner asks for another chance, listen to what they propose changing and decide based on your needs rather than pressure.","b":"A second chance is optional; you can acknowledge their regret while deciding that the relationship has reached its end."},{"a":"If you are the one asking for another chance, accept that your partner may say no and focus on taking responsibility without bargaining.","b":"Show change because it is the right thing to do, not as a transaction that guarantees reconciliation or renewed access."},{"a":"After a breakup, reduce contact if ongoing messages keep reopening the wound, and explain the boundary once if it is safe to do so.","b":"No-contact or limited contact can create room to recover; practical matters can be handled through a clear, narrow channel when necessary."},{"a":"If you share friends with an ex, avoid forcing people to take sides and agree on respectful boundaries for shared gatherings.","b":"You can ask friends for sensitivity without controlling their relationships; choose the events and contact that support your own recovery."},{"a":"Do not use a new relationship to provoke an ex or avoid processing the ending; give yourself space to understand what happened.","b":"Moving forward is personal, not a performance; take the time you need before making another commitment."},{"a":"If you are dating again after a difficult relationship, share relevant boundaries at a pace that feels safe rather than disclosing everything at once.","b":"You are allowed to have a past without making it your opening introduction; notice whether a new person respects your pace and choices."},{"a":"When you begin dating someone with children, understand that the child's needs and existing parenting arrangements come first.","b":"Build trust gradually and avoid stepping into a parental role before the child and their caregiver are ready."},{"a":"If you and your partner have different expectations about marriage, ask what marriage represents to each of you before deciding what to do.","b":"The ceremony, legal commitment, family meaning, and daily partnership may carry different weight; clarify the reasons beneath each person's view."},{"a":"If you are not interested in marriage, say so honestly rather than allowing a partner to plan around an assumption that you will change.","b":"You can love someone and still want a different future; clarity now is kinder than postponing a fundamental incompatibility."},{"a":"When deciding whether to stay, assess the relationship's present pattern, not only its best memories or its most difficult week.","b":"Look for respect, safety, mutual effort, and willingness to repair; make a decision from the overall reality rather than a single emotional moment."},{"a":"If you choose to stay through a difficult season, agree on what support and change you both expect and when you will review progress.","b":"Commitment should not mean tolerating indefinite harm; define what improvement looks like and keep your right to reconsider."},{"a":"If your partner's behaviour improves, acknowledge the effort while allowing trust to rebuild at a pace supported by consistency.","b":"Positive change deserves recognition, but one good week does not erase a long pattern; keep evaluating actions over time."},{"a":"When your own behaviour has damaged the relationship, seek help to change the pattern instead of asking your partner to keep absorbing it.","b":"Accountability includes learning new skills, accepting consequences, and respecting the other person's choice about whether to continue."},{"a":"If you both want to continue but keep repeating the same conflict, agree on one small experiment and review whether it helps.","b":"Make change measurable: choose a specific behaviour, practise it for an agreed period, and discuss what improved or still needs work."},{"a":"A strong relationship is not conflict-free; it is one where disagreement can happen without fear, contempt, or loss of respect.","b":"The quality of repair matters more than pretending everything is fine; look for honest listening, responsibility, and changed behaviour after conflict."},{"a":"Do not mistake intensity for compatibility; pay attention to whether the relationship is dependable when excitement gives way to ordinary life.","b":"Strong feelings can begin a connection, but shared values, respect, and practical reliability determine whether it can be sustained."},{"a":"If someone repeatedly says they will change but makes no effort, treat the gap between promise and behaviour as meaningful information.","b":"You can believe that a person wants to improve and still decide that the current pattern is not acceptable for your life."},{"a":"When you feel unsure about your standards, write down the behaviours that help you feel safe, respected, and able to be yourself.","b":"Standards are most useful when they describe how you expect to be treated, not when they become a checklist for controlling another person."},{"a":"If you have different needs for privacy, negotiate what is shared and what remains personal instead of assuming closeness means total access.","b":"Healthy intimacy includes trust and individuality; agree on transparency around shared matters while preserving each person's private space."},{"a":"If a partner makes you feel guilty for maintaining friendships or interests, explain that independence is part of a healthy life.","b":"A request for closeness should not require isolation; seek support if someone repeatedly pressures you to abandon your community."},{"a":"When you are unsure whether to continue, talk with a trusted, balanced person who can help you examine the facts without deciding for you.","b":"Outside perspective can help you see patterns, but the decision remains yours; choose support that respects your autonomy and safety."},{"a":"A relationship works best when both people can be honest about needs, limits, and changing circumstances without fear of punishment.","b":"Mutual respect is visible in everyday choices: listening, keeping agreements, sharing responsibility, and making room for each person's agency."}]};
